A||N||3||bHooper||Removeing Gt Ground effects||||5
Z||000000||bHooper||09-30-1999||07:40 AM||||What is the best way to remove GT Ground effects?  I do not want to damage my car, but I do want to remove the pannels so that I can work on the car easier.<BR>Thanks,<BR>Brian<BR><p>[This message has been edited by bHooper (edited 09-30-1999).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||CUTTER||09-30-1999||10:28 PM||||The ground effects have bolts holding them on. Just take the bolts off and they should come right off. Hope this helps,<BR>      Shane||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||10-01-1999||08:56 AM||||I haven't noticed any bolt's.  I have noticed what look like plastic rivets that are part of the ground effect, protruding into the backside of the fender, and an aluminum retaining plate around them.  ??????   I don't believe that I want to break these plastic pins as they appear to be part of the bodywork......much like the plastic pins on a plastic model car......||||reg||9
Z||000003||Punkndrublic||10-01-1999||07:37 PM||||I helped strip a fiero to the frame about 2 months ago, I got the unfortunate job of taking the ground effects off. There are no bolts, at least on that one. There were large metal rivets holding it on all across it, if I remember right. Me and some other guy finally decided to drill them out, even more fun, then used some fancy rivet removing tool someone had. The rest is more or less simple but any mechanically inclined individual won't breack anything, it just takes time especailly if you haven't done it before. One more thing, be careful the drill can slip easily and there goes a perfect ground effect.||||reg||1
